}5w & =5[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot 1 \\\\ 5\\cdot (-1) \\\\ 5\\cdot 3 \\\\ 5\\cdot (-4)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 15 \\\\ -20\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342113 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342113 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73941" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342113 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342113 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $c=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $d=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].$ What is $(-\\frac{1}{3}c)\\cdot d?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342113 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9d756465-9173-5daf-942c-7abdf0168d3c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342113 #edb/ref 1074 "First, multiplying each component of $c$ by $-\\frac{1}{3}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}c & =-\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the dot product, we add up the products of corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-\\frac{1}{3}c)\\cdot d & =0\\cdot 9+2\\cdot 4+(-4)\\cdot (-2)+(-3)\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =0+8+8-3-6 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342114 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341455 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342114 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73948" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342114 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342114 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $v=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 2x \\\\ 4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}3x \\\\ -3 \\\\ x \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\nIf $v\\cdot w=-2$ and $x>0$, find the value of $x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342114 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e030b11b-7d8c-5bb3-95d3-2268959b30a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342114 #edb/ref 1074 "Starting from the equation $v\\cdot w=-2,$ we compute the dot product in terms of $x$ and then solve for $x$ in the resulting equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot w & =-2 \\\\ x\\cdot 3x+2x\\cdot (-3)+4\\cdot x+(-5)\\cdot 2 & =-2 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-6x+4x-10 & =-2 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-2x-8 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(-2)\\pm \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-4(3)(-8)}}{2(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{100}}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm 10}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\pm 5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get two solutions, ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{4}{3}$ and ${x}_{2}=2.$ Since we need $x>0$, our final answer is $x=2$ only." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342115 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342115 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73949" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342115 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342115 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $l=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ x \\\\ 2 \\\\ x \\\\ 2x\\end{aligned}]$ and $m=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ x \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$\nIf $l\\cdot m=3$ and $x>3$, find the value of $x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342115 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "64baab11-7178-5faa-a751-966ea0fbee6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342115 #edb/ref 1074 "Starting from the equation $l\\cdot m=3,$ we compute the dot product in terms of $x$ and then solve for $x$ in the resulting equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l\\cdot m & =3 \\\\ 5\\cdot 1+x\\cdot x+2\\cdot 4+x\\cdot (-1)+2x\\cdot (-3) & =3 \\\\ 5+{x}^{2}+8-x-6x & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2}-7x+13 & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2}-7x+10 & =0 \\\\ (x-5)(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe get two solutions, ${x}_{1}=5$ and ${x}_{2}=2.$ Since we need $x>3$, our final answer is $x=5$ only." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391769 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186391770 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391769 #edb/ref 1069 "q-34158" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391769 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/r